Fig. 5 shows suction pressure distributions in the vertical direction when a water jet is spouted from a Cone nozzle or from V-jet nozzles. As shown in Fig. 5, the V-jet nozzle has a greater ejection effect than the Cone nozzle, providing a greater gas inflow rate. When using the V-jet nozzle. a space which is formed between adjoining jet curtains allows gas to flow more easily. In contrast, the Cone nozzle has no space between jet curtains. thus there is less of a gas inflow stream. Fig. 6 shows the disintegrating behaviour of a hot metal stream. The photo shows that tho hot metal stream has already been split by an inflow gas stream before it collides with the water jet. Fig. 5 also shows schematically the initial site of the split in the hot metal stream. The hot metal splits at the upper part of the jet intersection in both nozzles, but the V-jet nozzle has a higher splitting position. To investigate the effects of the gas inflow rate, an inner sleeve was installed at the water jet passage. and experiments were conducted with the V-jet nozzle. Fig. 7 shows the relationship between the mean particle 'diameter of the produced powders and the suction pressure which is considered to be a parameter representing the gas inflow rate. With the decrease in suction pressure, that is. the gas inflow rate. the mean particle size of produced powders increases. Fig. 8 shows the relatiqnshlp between the increase of ~he oxygen contents of powders and tho suction pressure. With the decrease in the gas inflow rate, the oxygen content also decreases.
